  "slug": "amino-acid-6d41",
  "summary": "New work pairs protein language models, matrix factorization, and standardized pipelines to mine resistance, virulence, and prophage content from microbial genomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is extracting functional structure from microbial genomic data at scale. A protein language model with ensemble learning identifies over 46,000 putative environmental beta-lactamases from metagenomes, with inverse folding used to assess their mutational potential. Non-negative matrix factorization of 1,398 S. aureus genomes splits the accessory pangenome into 24 additive gene modules separating lineage from adaptation and virulence. PHI packages prophage identification, quality assessment, host prediction, and functional annotation in a Galaxy workflow with standardized viral-genomics reporting. gcPathogen 2.0 expands a multi-omics database for pathogen surveillance and One Health research. The new element is the combination of learned or decomposition-based methods with reproducible, shared infrastructure; the items differ in organism and scale, and do not all share a modeling component.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-14"
}
